sections.s2_technical:
Consistent hashing places both cache nodes and keys on a conceptual ring. A key is stored on the first node clockwise from its hash. When a node is added or removed, only the keys between it and its neighbour move, roughly a fraction of one over the number of nodes, instead of remapping everything. Virtual nodes smooth out uneven distribution by giving each physical node many points on the ring.

```java
// Consistent hashing: find the node owning a key
public String nodeFor(String key) {
    int hash = hash(key);
    // first ring entry with hash >= key hash, wrapping around
    Map.Entry<Integer,String> e = ring.ceilingEntry(hash);
    return (e != null ? e : ring.firstEntry()).getValue();
}
```

Eviction decides what to drop when memory is full. Least-recently-used evicts the entry untouched for the longest time, which suits workloads with temporal locality. Least-frequently-used evicts the entry accessed the fewest times, which suits workloads where some keys are permanently hot. A time-to-live expires entries after a fixed duration regardless of access.

Invalidation keeps the cache consistent with the database. The simplest is a short time-to-live so stale data self-heals. Event-based invalidation deletes the cache entry the moment the underlying record changes, which is more precise. Write policies tie writes to the cache and database. Write-through writes to the cache and the database together, so the cache is always current but writes are a bit slower. Write-behind writes to the cache immediately and flushes to the database asynchronously, which is faster but risks losing recent writes if a node crashes before flushing, so it is unsuitable for money.

```java
// Write-through: update cache and DB together (safe for balances)
public void writeThrough(String key, String value) {
    database.save(key, value);   // durable first
    cache.put(key, value);       // then cache reflects it
}
```

Cache stampede, also called the thundering herd, happens when a very popular key expires and thousands of requests simultaneously miss the cache and all hit the database at once. Prevention techniques include a lock or single-flight so only one request rebuilds the value while others wait, and staggered or probabilistic early expiration so the key is refreshed before it fully expires. A senior nuance is that in banking you lean toward write-through and event-based invalidation for anything resembling a balance, accepting slightly slower writes in exchange for never serving a wrong number.

